Advanced Organization
31. Custom Hooks

A custom hook is a JavaScript function that contains reusable React logic.

Students have already used built-in hooks such as:

useState()
useEffect()

Sometimes the same logic is needed in multiple components. Instead of copying the code, we can create our own hook.

For example, suppose several components need to fetch data.

Instead of repeating:

const [data, setData] = useState([]);
const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);

useEffect(() => {
  async function getData() {
    const response = await fetch(url);
    const result = await response.json();

    setData(result);
    setLoading(false);
  }

  getData();
}, []);

we could create:

function useFetch(url) {
  const [data, setData] = useState([]);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);

  useEffect(() => {
    async function getData() {
      const response = await fetch(url);
      const result = await response.json();

      setData(result);
      setLoading(false);
    }

    getData();
  }, [url]);

  return { data, loading };
}

Then a component can use it:

function Products() {
  const { data, loading } = useFetch("/products");

  if (loading) {
    return <p>Loading...</p>;
  }

  return (
    <div>
      {data.map(product => (
        <p key={product.id}>{product.name}</p>
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}

The important idea is:

Normal function
    ↓
Reusable calculation/logic

Custom hook
    ↓
Reusable React logic

Custom hooks usually start with use.

Examples:

useFetch()
useForm()
useWindowSize()
useLocalStorage()

The student does not need to make complicated hooks at first. The main purpose is to avoid repeating the same React logic.

32. Context

Context allows data to be shared between many components without manually passing props through every component.

For example:

App
│
├── Navbar
│
└── Main
    │
    └── Product

Suppose App has a username:

const [username, setUsername] = useState("Denny");

Normally, if Product needs the username, we might pass it through props:

App
 ↓ props
Main
 ↓ props
Product

This can become annoying when there are many levels.

Context allows us to make the data available to components that need it.

For example:

import { createContext } from "react";

const UserContext = createContext();

Then we provide the data:

<UserContext.Provider value={username}>
  <App />
</UserContext.Provider>

A component can then access it with useContext():

const username = useContext(UserContext);

The basic idea is:

Without Context:

App
 ↓
Page
 ↓
Section
 ↓
Component


With Context:

        Context
       ↙   ↓   ↘
    Page Section Component

Context is useful for information that many parts of the application need.

Examples include:

Logged-in user
Theme
Language
Shopping cart
Application settings

It should not automatically be used for every piece of state. Normal props are still useful when data only needs to move between nearby components.

33. Reusable Components

A reusable component is a component designed so that it can be used in multiple places.

For example, instead of creating three different buttons:

<button>Add</button>
<button>Delete</button>
<button>Edit</button>

we could create a reusable component:

function Button({ children }) {
  return <button>{children}</button>;
}

Then:

<Button>Add</Button>
<Button>Edit</Button>
<Button>Delete</Button>

The component can also receive props:

function Button({ children, onClick }) {
  return (
    <button onClick={onClick}>
      {children}
    </button>
  );
}

Now different parts of the application can use the same component with different behavior.

Another example is a reusable Card:

function Card({ title, description }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>{title}</h2>
      <p>{description}</p>
    </div>
  );
}

Then:

<Card
  title="Minecraft"
  description="A sandbox game"
/>

<Card
  title="Terraria"
  description="An adventure game"
/>

The important question when creating a component is:

"Could I use this component somewhere else?"

If yes, it may be a good candidate for a reusable component.

34. Project / Folder Organization

As a React project becomes larger, putting everything into one folder or one file becomes difficult to manage.

A small project might start like:

src/
├── App.jsx
├── Home.jsx
├── Products.jsx
└── About.jsx

A larger project can be organized into folders:

src/
├── components/
│   ├── Button.jsx
│   ├── Card.jsx
│   └── Navbar.jsx
│
├── pages/
│   ├── Home.jsx
│   ├── Products.jsx
│   └── ProductDetail.jsx
│
├── hooks/
│   └── useFetch.jsx
│
├── context/
│   └── UserContext.jsx
│
├── services/
│   └── productApi.js
│
├── App.jsx
└── main.jsx

Each folder has a general purpose.

components

Reusable visual components.

Button
Card
Navbar
Modal
pages

Components that represent whole pages.

Home
Products
ProductDetail
About
hooks

Custom hooks.

useFetch
useForm
context

Context-related code.

UserContext
CartContext
services

Code that communicates with APIs or handles data-related operations.

For example:

export async function getProducts() {
  const response = await fetch("/products");
  return response.json();
}

This organization makes it easier to find code later.

35. Separating UI and Data Logic

This is an important idea when projects become larger.

UI logic is concerned with what the user sees.

For example:

function ProductCard({ product }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>{product.name}</h2>
      <p>${product.price}</p>
    </div>
  );
}

This component mainly cares about displaying the product.

Data logic is concerned with getting or changing data.

For example:

async function getProducts() {
  const response = await fetch("/products");
  return response.json();
}

Instead of putting everything into one huge component:

ProductPage.jsx
    ↓
fetch
    ↓
state
    ↓
validation
    ↓
form
    ↓
UI
    ↓
buttons
    ↓
API

we can separate the responsibilities.

For example:

ProductPage
    ↓
uses data
    ↓
productApi.js
    ↓
fetches data

And:

ProductPage
    ↓
renders
    ↓
ProductCard

A possible structure:

pages/
    Products.jsx

components/
    ProductCard.jsx

services/
    productApi.js

productApi.js:

export async function getProducts() {
  const response = await fetch("/products");
  return response.json();
}

ProductCard.jsx:

function ProductCard({ product }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>{product.name}</h2>
      <p>{product.price}</p>
    </div>
  );
}

Products.jsx can then combine them.

The goal is not to completely separate every single line of code. The goal is to make each part have a clear responsibility.

